WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.079 --> 00:00:04.745
The reign of King Henry VIII of England was plagued by controversy.

2
00:00:04.746 --> 00:00:14.069
Catherine of Aragon, Henry's wife, gave birth to six children, but only Mary survived through infancy, leaving Henry with no male heir.

3
00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:21.339
He blamed Catherine and sought to dissolve their union in order to marry another woman—one who could bear him a son.

4
00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:31.674
The Roman Catholic pope, however, refused to annul his marriage, so Henry broke the English Church away from the Catholic Church, led by the pope in Rome.

5
00:00:31.675 --> 00:00:41.205
In many nations, new Protestant churches were being established as the Reformation—a religious and political movement defying Rome—swept across Europe.

6
00:00:41.206 --> 00:00:47.259
In England, the popes' claim to jurisdiction over the country had been a long-running source of dispute.

7
00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:55.489
In a pivotal moment of the English Reformation, Henry's break with Rome resulted in the formation of a new church, the Church of England.

8
00:00:55.490 --> 00:01:02.428
Henry declared his marriage to Catherine invalid, and England's new church recognized the divorce.

9
00:01:02.429 --> 00:01:08.974
When Henry remarried, it was to Anne Boleyn, a well-known and influential woman in his court.

10
00:01:08.975 --> 00:01:16.696
As queen, she stripped Mary of her title and ordered her to serve Elizabeth, her own newborn daughter by Henry.

11
00:01:16.697 --> 00:01:22.472
But when Anne also failed to provide a male heir, Henry had her beheaded for adultery.

12
00:01:22.473 --> 00:01:29.787
Henry married again; this time, his wife, Jane Seymour, died soon after giving birth to a boy, Edward.

13
00:01:29.788 --> 00:01:36.015
But Edward seemed frail, and Henry remarried three more times in the hopes of fathering another son.

14
00:01:36.016 --> 00:01:39.553
None of these marriages, however, produced a male child.

15
00:01:39.554 --> 00:01:49.373
When Henry died in 1547, Edward became king. He had been raised Protestant, and he continued his father's break with Catholicism.

16
00:01:49.374 --> 00:01:59.206
However, Edward ruled for only six years before he died of tuberculosis; Mary, who had remained a devout Catholic, then took the throne.

17
00:01:59.207 --> 00:02:06.708
Mary was determined to repair relations with the pope and restore Catholicism as the official religion of England.

18
00:02:06.709 --> 00:02:13.096
To this end, she wed King Philip II of Spain, a champion of Catholicism in Europe.

19
00:02:13.097 --> 00:02:18.469
Many of her subjects, particularly the English Protestants, were unhappy with the marriage.

20
00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:24.691
Sir Thomas Wyatt the Younger, a soldier, organized a rebellion in the predominantly Protestant north.

21
00:02:24.692 --> 00:02:30.638
The rebellion moved into London and grew to more than three thousand men before it was stopped.

22
00:02:30.639 --> 00:02:36.017
After this rebellion, Mary began to fear that everyone was plotting against her.

23
00:02:36.018 --> 00:02:44.685
She even had her half sister, Elizabeth, imprisoned in the Tower of London while her ministers looked for a connection between Elizabeth and Wyatt.

24
00:02:44.686 --> 00:02:50.241
After two months, Mary released Elizabeth but kept her under house arrest.

25
00:02:50.242 --> 00:02:58.049
Mary's attempts to restore Catholicism in England led to the persecution of Protestants and other opponents of Catholicism.

26
00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:04.317
Thousands were burned at the stake for heresy, which earned the queen the unwelcome nickname "Bloody Mary."

27
00:03:04.318 --> 00:03:13.214
Her husband had returned to Spain soon after their marriage, and at his request, she supplied English soldiers to support Spain's war with France.

28
00:03:13.215 --> 00:03:22.098
England's involvement in this war led to the loss of the last English territory on the European continent, the port city of Calais.

29
00:03:22.099 --> 00:03:29.251
This loss did not endear Mary to her subjects. She became depressed and her health deteriorated.

30
00:03:29.252 --> 00:03:38.681
After only five years of rule, Mary died without leaving behind an heir. The next person in the line of succession was her half sister, Elizabeth.

31
00:03:38.682 --> 00:03:43.078
When Elizabeth assumed the throne, no one knew where her allegiances lay.

32
00:03:43.079 --> 00:03:52.312
Elizabeth had grown up Protestant but had attended Catholic services during Mary's reign. Elizabeth's competency to rule also came into question.

33
00:03:52.313 --> 00:04:01.911
It was a common belief at the time that women based decisions solely on their emotions, not reason or logic, and thus were not fit to command countries.

34
00:04:01.912 --> 00:04:07.594
Elizabeth, however, had been educated in classical languages, rhetoric, history, and philosophy.

35
00:04:07.595 --> 00:04:16.308
She responded to these objections by stating that, although she was female, she acquired a new, genderless body when she became queen.

36
00:04:16.309 --> 00:04:20.435
Elizabeth's rule over England was long, dramatic, and controversial.

37
00:04:20.436 --> 00:04:27.133
One of her first acts as queen was to reinstate the Church of England as the official religion of her kingdom.

38
00:04:27.134 --> 00:04:39.588
She passed the Act of Uniformity in 1559, which commanded that church services use the Protestant Book of Common Prayer, which was in English, whereas the Catholic Church still used Latin.

39
00:04:39.589 --> 00:04:45.578
Some Protestants were pleased with these developments, but others thought they did not go far enough.

40
00:04:45.579 --> 00:04:50.301
During her reign, Elizabeth dealt with rebellions led by Catholics.

41
00:04:50.302 --> 00:04:55.228
Several plots to assassinate her were discovered before they could be put into action.

42
00:04:55.229 --> 00:05:00.789
The Ridolfi Plot involved her cousin Mary Stuart—better known as Mary, Queen of Scots.

43
00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:05.883
Mary was a Catholic and therefore supported by the pope in her claim to the English crown.

44
00:05:05.884 --> 00:05:18.498
Roberto Ridolfi, an Italian banker living in London and a devout Catholic, believed that the only way to return Catholicism to England was to assassinate Elizabeth and put Mary Stuart on the throne.

45
00:05:18.499 --> 00:05:28.714
But Elizabeth's intelligence service uncovered the plot in 1571, and most of the guilty were put to death—although Elizabeth refused to execute Mary.

46
00:05:28.715 --> 00:05:36.597
Only after Mary Stuart was implicated in another assassination plot did Elizabeth agree to put her cousin to death.

47
00:05:36.598 --> 00:05:42.358
Despite the controversies of Elizabeth's reign, her subjects had many reasons to celebrate.

48
00:05:42.359 --> 00:05:49.292
The Elizabethan period later came to be known as the English Renaissance because the arts flourished during this time.

49
00:05:49.293 --> 00:05:54.236
William Shakespeare wrote most of his plays while Elizabeth was on the throne.

50
00:05:54.237 --> 00:06:00.338
She hired dozens of musicians and composers to create music for the court and the English people.

51
00:06:00.339 --> 00:06:09.212
There were military victories as well. Sir Francis Drake, a privateer supported by Elizabeth, led raids on Spanish ports and trading ships.

52
00:06:09.213 --> 00:06:24.009
And when Philip II of Spain, her former brother-in-law, attacked England in order to add it to the Spanish Empire and return it to Catholicism, his supposedly invincible navy, known as "the Armada," proved no match for the English fleet.

53
00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:28.703
Like her half sister, Mary, Elizabeth remained childless throughout her reign.

54
00:06:28.704 --> 00:06:35.314
Many of her advisors tried to convince her to take a husband and produce an heir to resolve the issue of succession.

55
00:06:35.315 --> 00:06:42.422
But Elizabeth refused to marry; she proclaimed that she was married to her kingdom and responsible for its people.

56
00:06:42.423 --> 00:06:51.519
These were not empty words. Before the English victory over the Spanish Armada, Elizabeth had personally reviewed her troops in spite of the danger.

57
00:06:51.520 --> 00:07:02.906
When her advisors protested, Elizabeth responded by saying, "I know I have the body of a weak and feeble woman, but I have the heart and stomach of a king, and of a king of England too."

58
00:07:02.907 --> 00:07:11.503
Beloved by her people, Good Queen Bess, as she became known, ruled for over 44 years until her death in 1603.

59
00:07:11.504 --> 00:07:20.336
One might expect that their common upbringing would lead Bloody Mary and Good Queen Bess to hold similar values and adopt similar ways of ruling the country.

60
00:07:20.337 --> 00:07:24.884
The reigns of these two queens, however, could not have been more different.

61
00:07:24.885 --> 00:07:29.934
Their responses to events during their reigns had contrasting effects on the country.

62
00:07:29.935 --> 00:07:35.870
But even though they differed hugely in both personalities and beliefs, they shared a love of England.

63
00:07:35.871 --> 00:07:48.738
Perhaps this is why they are interred together in Westminster Abbey under this inscription: "Partners both in throne and grave, here rest we two sisters, Elizabeth and Mary, in the hope of one resurrection."

